I need to check the carousel CSS - the `.carousel__slide` has a `min-width` set in CSS but also inline in the HTML. The inline style will override the CSS. Let me check:

In CSS:
```css
.carousel__slide {
  min-width: calc(33.333% - 16px);
  ...
}
```

In the HTML (index.html), each slide has:
```html
style="...;min-width:calc(33.333% - 16px);"
```

The inline styles have both the gradient background AND min-width. The problem is that in the JS, the `getSlidesPerView()` function returns the number of slides visible, and `updateCarousel()` calculates the slide width from `slides[0].offsetWidth + 16`. But the CSS has a `margin: 0 8px` on the carousel slides, which means the gap is 16px between slides.

Actually, the issue is that the inline min-width on the slides will override the CSS responsive breakpoints. The JS handles the responsive behavior by calculating the offset, but the min-width of the slides stays at `calc(33.333% - 16px)` on all screen sizes because it's inline.

